1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,716
It is Friday night.

2
00:00:01,716 --> 00:00:02,804
A server is slow,

3
00:00:02,804 --> 00:00:04,916
and an engineer fixes it by hand.

4
00:00:04,916 --> 00:00:05,876
A setting here,

5
00:00:05,876 --> 00:00:06,900
a package there.

6
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:12,698
Nobody writes it down. Six months later, the server dies. And nobody can rebuild it.

7
00:00:13,248 --> 00:00:15,358
A second team has the same problem.

8
00:00:15,358 --> 00:00:17,769
But their server is described in a file,

9
00:00:17,769 --> 00:00:19,819
and a new one is ready in minutes.

10
00:00:20,369 --> 00:00:22,417
Today: infrastructure as code,

11
00:00:22,417 --> 00:00:24,123
configuration management,

12
00:00:24,123 --> 00:00:25,147
and automation.

13
00:00:25,147 --> 00:00:27,946
The three ideas that make servers boring,

14
00:00:27,946 --> 00:00:28,902
in a good way.

15
00:00:30,452 --> 00:00:31,881
Here is what to expect.

16
00:00:32,431 --> 00:00:35,994
One: infrastructure as code, and why a file beats a memory.

17
00:00:36,544 --> 00:00:41,088
Two: configuration management, and one magic word: idempotent.

18
00:00:41,638 --> 00:00:46,801
Three: drift, and how to find it and fix it. Then automation, and which tools do what.

19
00:00:47,351 --> 00:00:48,520
Four: a live demo.

20
00:00:48,520 --> 00:00:50,794
We run a naive script and break it,

21
00:00:50,794 --> 00:00:52,093
then run a safe one,

22
00:00:52,093 --> 00:00:53,588
cause drift on purpose,

23
00:00:53,588 --> 00:00:54,497
and repair it.

24
00:00:55,047 --> 00:00:58,375
This is lesson three of the DevOps fundamentals domain.

25
00:01:00,425 --> 00:01:03,028
Start with infrastructure as code. IaC.

26
00:01:03,578 --> 00:01:05,492
Infrastructure is the servers,

27
00:01:05,492 --> 00:01:06,321
the networks,

28
00:01:06,321 --> 00:01:07,215
the databases,

29
00:01:07,215 --> 00:01:08,427
the load balancers.

30
00:01:08,427 --> 00:01:10,724
The things your application runs on.

31
00:01:11,274 --> 00:01:13,230
Instead of clicking in a console,

32
00:01:13,230 --> 00:01:14,830
you describe them in files.

33
00:01:14,830 --> 00:01:15,956
The files are text,

34
00:01:15,956 --> 00:01:17,141
so they live in Git.

35
00:01:17,691 --> 00:01:19,085
That gives you history,

36
00:01:19,085 --> 00:01:19,510
review,

37
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:20,298
and rollback.

38
00:01:20,298 --> 00:01:21,813
Who changed the firewall,

39
00:01:21,813 --> 00:01:22,116
when,

40
00:01:22,116 --> 00:01:22,601
and why?

41
00:01:22,601 --> 00:01:23,814
It is in the commit.

42
00:01:24,364 --> 00:01:26,614
And you can build the same thing again,

43
00:01:26,614 --> 00:01:27,653
in another region,

44
00:01:27,653 --> 00:01:28,750
or another account.

45
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:29,846
From the same file.

46
00:01:31,396 --> 00:01:35,343
Now the big idea, in a picture. The same code, many servers.

47
00:01:35,893 --> 00:01:36,387
By hand,

48
00:01:36,387 --> 00:01:38,552
every server is a little different.

49
00:01:38,552 --> 00:01:40,469
Engineers call them snowflakes,

50
00:01:40,469 --> 00:01:42,015
because no two are alike.

51
00:01:42,565 --> 00:01:47,941
With code, you stamp out copies. Four servers, built from one file, and all identical.

52
00:01:48,491 --> 00:01:49,318
Need a fifth?

53
00:01:49,318 --> 00:01:50,526
Run the code again.

54
00:01:50,526 --> 00:01:51,544
A server breaks?

55
00:01:51,544 --> 00:01:53,261
Do not repair it for hours.

56
00:01:53,261 --> 00:01:53,960
Replace it,

57
00:01:53,960 --> 00:01:55,169
from the same file.

58
00:01:55,719 --> 00:01:57,048
Servers stop being pets,

59
00:01:57,048 --> 00:01:58,044
and become cattle.

60
00:01:58,044 --> 00:01:59,207
You do not name them,

61
00:01:59,207 --> 00:02:00,647
and you do not nurse them.

62
00:02:02,197 --> 00:02:06,186
Next, configuration management. It answers a different question.

63
00:02:06,736 --> 00:02:08,504
IaC creates the machine.

64
00:02:08,504 --> 00:02:11,904
Configuration management sets it up: the packages,

65
00:02:11,904 --> 00:02:12,584
the users,

66
00:02:12,584 --> 00:02:13,468
the settings,

67
00:02:13,468 --> 00:02:14,352
the services.

68
00:02:14,902 --> 00:02:16,557
You do not write the steps.

69
00:02:16,557 --> 00:02:20,296
You describe the end result you want: this package installed,

70
00:02:20,296 --> 00:02:22,013
this file with this content.

71
00:02:22,013 --> 00:02:23,606
That is the desired state.

72
00:02:24,156 --> 00:02:29,020
The tool compares the desired state with reality, and changes only what is different.

73
00:02:29,570 --> 00:02:32,725
That brings us to the magic word: idempotent.

74
00:02:32,725 --> 00:02:33,566
Run it once,

75
00:02:33,566 --> 00:02:34,477
or ten times,

76
00:02:34,477 --> 00:02:36,370
and the result is the same.

77
00:02:36,370 --> 00:02:38,402
A second run changes nothing.

78
00:02:38,952 --> 00:02:39,600
Without it,

79
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:42,074
running a script twice can break a server.

80
00:02:42,074 --> 00:02:42,545
With it,

81
00:02:42,545 --> 00:02:43,959
you can run it any time,

82
00:02:43,959 --> 00:02:44,371
safely.

83
00:02:45,921 --> 00:02:47,457
Now the enemy: drift.

84
00:02:48,007 --> 00:02:53,362
Your server matches the code today. And then, at night, someone edits a file by hand.

85
00:02:53,912 --> 00:02:57,880
Now the server no longer matches the code. That gap is called drift.

86
00:02:58,430 --> 00:02:59,545
Drift is dangerous,

87
00:02:59,545 --> 00:03:01,307
because nobody knows about it.

88
00:03:01,307 --> 00:03:02,716
The code says one thing,

89
00:03:02,716 --> 00:03:04,360
and production does another.

90
00:03:04,910 --> 00:03:06,390
The cure has two steps.

91
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:06,775
First,

92
00:03:06,775 --> 00:03:09,605
detect it: compare the server with the code.

93
00:03:09,605 --> 00:03:11,664
Then fix it: run the code again,

94
00:03:11,664 --> 00:03:13,593
and the server converges back.

95
00:03:14,143 --> 00:03:17,151
And the rule for the team: change the code, not the server.

96
00:03:18,701 --> 00:03:20,493
Third idea: automation.

97
00:03:21,043 --> 00:03:22,726
If you do a task by hand,

98
00:03:22,726 --> 00:03:23,130
twice,

99
00:03:23,130 --> 00:03:24,274
and it is boring,

100
00:03:24,274 --> 00:03:25,688
and it follows steps,

101
00:03:25,688 --> 00:03:26,899
it is a candidate.

102
00:03:26,899 --> 00:03:27,842
Build the app.

103
00:03:27,842 --> 00:03:28,784
Run the tests.

104
00:03:28,784 --> 00:03:29,861
Create a server.

105
00:03:29,861 --> 00:03:31,006
Apply the config.

106
00:03:31,556 --> 00:03:33,552
Good automation is repeatable,

107
00:03:33,552 --> 00:03:36,214
because it runs the same way every time.

108
00:03:36,214 --> 00:03:36,947
It is fast,

109
00:03:36,947 --> 00:03:38,344
and it is documented,

110
00:03:38,344 --> 00:03:41,006
because the script is the documentation.

111
00:03:41,556 --> 00:03:42,592
But start small.

112
00:03:42,592 --> 00:03:44,275
Automate the most painful,

113
00:03:44,275 --> 00:03:45,893
most frequent task first.

114
00:03:45,893 --> 00:03:49,194
Automating a bad process only makes it fail faster.

115
00:03:49,744 --> 00:03:52,918
Manual work that is repetitive has a name: toil.

116
00:03:52,918 --> 00:03:54,638
The aim is to remove toil,

117
00:03:54,638 --> 00:03:57,018
and spend the time on better things.

118
00:03:58,568 --> 00:04:01,512
Which tools do what? A quick map, for the road ahead.

119
00:04:02,062 --> 00:04:07,673
Terraform creates infrastructure: servers, networks, cloud resources. That is IaC.

120
00:04:08,223 --> 00:04:10,133
Ansible configures machines,

121
00:04:10,133 --> 00:04:10,883
over SSH,

122
00:04:10,883 --> 00:04:12,179
from a description.

123
00:04:12,179 --> 00:04:14,431
That is configuration management.

124
00:04:14,981 --> 00:04:19,245
Docker and Kubernetes go one step further: you ship a whole image,

125
00:04:19,245 --> 00:04:20,215
and replace it,

126
00:04:20,215 --> 00:04:21,701
instead of changing it.

127
00:04:22,251 --> 00:04:22,757
Jenkins,

128
00:04:22,757 --> 00:04:23,958
and GitHub Actions,

129
00:04:23,958 --> 00:04:25,855
run all of this automatically,

130
00:04:25,855 --> 00:04:26,867
on every commit.

131
00:04:26,867 --> 00:04:29,333
We will meet each one in this bootcamp.

132
00:04:29,883 --> 00:04:33,190
The tools change. The ideas in this lesson stay the same.

133
00:04:34,740 --> 00:04:39,028
Go to the demo folder.

134
00:04:40,578 --> 00:04:42,477
Look around.

135
00:04:43,027 --> 00:04:45,693
Two scripts, and a folder with the desired state.

136
00:04:46,243 --> 00:04:50,851
The desired state.

137
00:04:51,401 --> 00:04:56,329
Two settings: port eighty eighty, and mode prod. This is what the server should look like.

138
00:04:56,879 --> 00:05:02,917
The naive way.

139
00:05:03,467 --> 00:05:04,960
Run it again.

140
00:05:06,010 --> 00:05:10,127
Look at the result.

141
00:05:10,677 --> 00:05:11,210
Doubled.

142
00:05:11,210 --> 00:05:12,676
The same two settings,

143
00:05:12,676 --> 00:05:13,075
twice.

144
00:05:13,075 --> 00:05:15,207
Running it twice broke the file.

145
00:05:15,207 --> 00:05:17,205
This script is not idempotent.

146
00:05:17,755 --> 00:05:21,467
The safe way.

147
00:05:22,017 --> 00:05:26,668
Changed, and changed. It created both files, to match the desired state.

148
00:05:27,218 --> 00:05:28,114
Run it again.

149
00:05:29,764 --> 00:05:35,332
Ok, and ok. Nothing changed, because nothing was different. That is idempotent.

150
00:05:35,882 --> 00:05:40,767
Cause drift.

151
00:05:41,317 --> 00:05:44,859
Detect it.

152
00:05:45,409 --> 00:05:47,926
Drift. The server no longer matches the code.

153
00:05:48,476 --> 00:05:52,679
Fix it.

154
00:05:53,229 --> 00:05:57,687
Changed, only the one file that was wrong. The other file was left alone.

155
00:05:58,237 --> 00:06:01,352
Check again.

156
00:06:01,902 --> 00:06:03,203
Clean. No drift.

157
00:06:03,753 --> 00:06:07,593
Write it down.

158
00:06:15,043 --> 00:06:17,539
Check the file.

159
00:06:19,089 --> 00:06:22,353
Your practice task: break a script, then do it the safe way.

160
00:06:22,903 --> 00:06:26,423
One: run the naive script twice, and look at the doubled file.

161
00:06:26,973 --> 00:06:32,114
Two: run the apply script twice. The first run changes, the second says ok.

162
00:06:32,664 --> 00:06:37,123
Three: edit the server file by hand, and run the check. It must say drift.

163
00:06:37,673 --> 00:06:42,814
Four: run apply again, and check again. It must say clean. Then write your three lines.

164
00:06:44,864 --> 00:06:48,085
Where to find the demo files. Pause here, and write it down.

165
00:06:48,635 --> 00:06:54,310
One: the repository, github.com/it-waves/devops-ai.

166
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:59,212
Two: the folder academy/lesson147-demo.

167
00:06:59,762 --> 00:07:01,067
Three: three scripts,

168
00:07:01,067 --> 00:07:01,439
naive,

169
00:07:01,439 --> 00:07:01,812
apply,

170
00:07:01,812 --> 00:07:02,433
and check,

171
00:07:02,433 --> 00:07:03,489
a desired folder,

172
00:07:03,489 --> 00:07:05,415
and a read me with the answers.

173
00:07:05,965 --> 00:07:07,680
Four: clone the repository,

174
00:07:07,680 --> 00:07:08,951
and copy the folder.

175
00:07:08,951 --> 00:07:10,348
You need only a shell.

176
00:07:10,348 --> 00:07:11,555
Nothing to install.

177
00:07:12,105 --> 00:07:12,840
Five: again,

178
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:14,189
no lab in this domain.

179
00:07:14,189 --> 00:07:15,538
The practice is plain,

180
00:07:15,538 --> 00:07:17,929
and the answers are on the next screen.

181
00:07:19,979 --> 00:07:22,454
Check yourself. Six points. Tick each one.

182
00:07:23,004 --> 00:07:26,204
One: the naive script doubles the file on the second run.

183
00:07:26,754 --> 00:07:28,994
Two: apply changes on the first run.

184
00:07:29,544 --> 00:07:31,634
Three: and says ok on the second.

185
00:07:32,184 --> 00:07:34,915
Four: a manual edit makes the check say drift.

186
00:07:35,465 --> 00:07:38,281
Five: apply fixes it, and the check says clean.

187
00:07:38,831 --> 00:07:41,711
Six: your file has three lines, in your own words.

188
00:07:42,261 --> 00:07:44,736
All six, and you understand the three ideas.

189
00:07:46,786 --> 00:07:48,557
Your turn, on your own machine.

190
00:07:49,107 --> 00:07:50,941
One: copy the demo folder.

191
00:07:51,491 --> 00:07:54,606
Two: run the naive script twice, and see the problem.

192
00:07:55,156 --> 00:07:59,039
Three: run apply twice, then cause drift, detect it, and fix it.

193
00:07:59,589 --> 00:08:01,167
Four: write your three lines.

194
00:08:01,717 --> 00:08:03,286
Five: tick the six points.

195
00:08:03,286 --> 00:08:07,328
Then think about your own servers: is anything there built by hand?

196
00:08:07,878 --> 00:08:09,713
Pause the video, and begin now.

197
00:08:25,263 --> 00:08:26,138
Quick check.

198
00:08:26,688 --> 00:08:28,437
What is infrastructure as code?

199
00:08:28,987 --> 00:08:30,715
What does idempotent mean?

200
00:08:31,265 --> 00:08:33,228
What is drift, and how do you fix it?

201
00:08:33,778 --> 00:08:37,042
Name a tool for IaC, and one for configuration.

202
00:08:37,592 --> 00:08:39,850
Objective covered: MC16,

203
00:08:39,850 --> 00:08:42,763
objective three: infrastructure as code,

204
00:08:42,763 --> 00:08:44,584
configuration management,

205
00:08:44,584 --> 00:08:45,677
and automation.

206
00:08:46,227 --> 00:08:48,296
